009, the band began mixing the record with Phillipe Zdar at Motorbass, Zdar's studio in Paris. In January 2010, Two Door Cinema Club announced the track listing and details of the band's debut album in an interview with", "draft": "EMPTY_DRAFT", "draft_f1_vs_gold": 0.0, "raw_len": 259, "prompt_tokens": 264, "completion_tokens": 63, "retrieval_scores": [0.8702222108840942, 0.8698104619979858, 0.8361271023750305]}, {"t": 2, "action_type": "search", "query_or_answer": "Cowboy Junkies members", "obs_digest": "[1] Cowboy Junkies: Cowboy Junkies The Cowboy Junkies are an alternative country and folk rock band formed in Toronto, Ontario, Canada in 1985. The group was formed in 1985 by Alan Anton (bassist), Michael Timmins (songwriter, guitarist), Peter Timmins (drummer) and Margo Timmins (vocalist). The three Timminses are siblings, and Anton worked with Michael Timmins during their first couple of bands. John Timmins was initially a member of the band but left the group before the recording of their first album. The band line-up has never changed since, although they use several guest musicians on many of their albums, including multi-instrumentalist Jeff Bird\n[2] Cowboy Junkies: published seven of their live performances. There are four band members, all siblings except Anton, who created and performed in two prior bands with Michael Timmins. Since their second album the band has performed and recorded with multi-instrumentalist Jeff Bird, whose current role includes acoustic and electric mandolins, harmonica, percussion, and samples. Cowboy Junkies The Cowboy Junkies are an alternative country and folk rock band formed in Toronto, Ontario, Canada in 1985. The group was formed in 1985 by Alan Anton (bassist), Michael Timmins (songwriter, guitarist), Peter Timmins (drummer) and Margo Timmins (vocalist). The three Timminses are siblings, and Anton\n[3] Cowboy Junkies: best albums of 1988. The Cowboy Junkies have gone on to record a total of sixteen studio albums and five live albums, and remain an active band for over thirty years. Alan Anton and Michael Timmins, lifelong friends who met in kindergarten, formed their first band in high school. In 1979, influenced by the wave of post-Sex Pistols punk bands, they recruited drummer Geoff Railton and singer Liza Dawson-Whisker, and formed Hunger Project in Toronto. They moved to New York City, to Manhattan's Lower East Side. They performed at a variety of clubs. In 1981 the Hunger Project embarked on", "draft": "EMPTY_DRAFT", "draft_f1_vs_gold": 0.0, "raw_len": 222, "prompt_tokens": 843, "completion_tokens": 55, "retrieval_scores": [0.8639100790023804, 0.8472232818603516, 0.8381147980690002]}, {"t": 3, "action_type": "answer", "query_or_answer": "Cowboy Junkies", "obs_digest": "", "draft": "Cowboy Junkies", "draft_f1_vs_gold": 1.0, "raw_len": 292, "prompt_tokens": 1404, "completion_tokens": 75, "retrieval_scores": []}], "answered_at": 3, "forced_stop": false, "final_answer": "Cowboy Junkies", "final_f1": 1.0, "final_em": 1.0, "steps_used": 3, "total_steps_run": 3, "rollout": 0}
